var BANK_PARTS = BANK_PARTS || [];
BANK_PARTS.push([
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":1,"s":"In a coastal lab, mice given a kelp supplement gained more weight over eight weeks than mice in the same room that were not given it. The director concludes that the kelp supplement caused the extra weight gain.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["treats the supplement and the extra gain occurring together as sufficient to show that the supplement caused the gain","takes for granted that no mouse can gain weight unless it receives the kelp supplement","draws a conclusion about every coastal lab from results in a single room","infers that the group of mice has a trait shown for only one mouse","takes for granted that the result could not occur at all unless the factor behind the claim that the kelp supplement caused the extra weight gain was present"],"a":0,"e":"The only link offered is that the two things moved together, and the argument treats that as causation. The attractive choice — takes for granted that no mouse can gain weight unless it receives the kelp supplement — loses because it names a mistake arguments often make, using this argument's subject, but it is not the step this argument takes."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Equivocation","d":3,"s":"A critic called the quartet's recording \"live,\" meaning the players performed together in one room. The prize rules reserve the live-recording prize for a recording made at a public concert. The producer concludes that the recording is eligible because the critic called it live.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["appeals to the critic as an authority on which recordings deserve the prize","infers eligibility merely because the review and the rules appeared in the same month","generalizes from this recording to every recording the quartet has made","uses the critic's sense of \"live\" as if it were the prize rules' sense of \"live\"","rejects the prize rules because the organizers might gain from a sold-out concert"],"a":3,"e":"One sense of \"live\" is a shared room and the other is a public concert. The eligibility claim treats them as the same sense. The attractive choice — appeals to the critic as an authority on which recordings deserve the prize — loses because the producer does not treat the critic as a prize judge. The producer treats the critic's word as if it satisfied a rule that uses the same word for a public concert."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Circular reasoning","d":3,"s":"The new soil test is accurate because it agrees with the farmer's notebook, and the notebook is accurate because it agrees with the new soil test.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the notebook caused the test results merely because the two agree","lets the test and the notebook certify each other","draws a conclusion about every soil test from this one test","treats a smaller share of matching rows as a smaller number of matching rows","appeals to the farmer's standing in the county"],"a":1,"e":"Each source is the only support offered for the other, so neither accuracy claim is independently established. The attractive choice — infers that the notebook caused the test results merely because the two agree — loses because agreement is being used as mutual proof of accuracy, not as a claim that the notebook produced the results. A causal reading adds a conclusion the argument does not draw."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":1,"s":"The outdoor stage has held the summer opera every year for fifteen years. The producer concludes that it will hold this year's opera.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the stage caused the opera's artistic success merely because the two occurred together in this instance","treats one opera company as representative of every arts group","appeals to a famous singer's endorsement of the stage","lets this year's contract and last year's contract certify each other","projects that run of success forward without showing that this year's load matches the earlier ones"],"a":4,"e":"Fifteen successful years support this year only if this production is relevantly like the earlier ones, and that likeness is not given. The attractive choice — infers that the stage caused the opera's artistic success merely because the two occurred together in this instance — loses because the argument does not claim the stage caused artistic success. It claims a structure that held past productions will hold the next one."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":1,"s":"Adding limestone to the east meadow reduced soil acidity there. Therefore, adding limestone to the bog will reduce soil acidity in the bog.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the limestone caused the meadow result merely because the two happened in the same season","treats each plot in the meadow as having a feature that only the whole meadow has","transfers a result from the meadow to the bog without establishing a relevant likeness","appeals to a celebrity gardener's endorsement of limestone","treats the absence of a contrary bog study as proof the bog will respond"],"a":2,"e":"A meadow fix is handed to a bog. Nothing shows the soils match in the way that made the limestone work. The attractive choice — infers that the limestone caused the meadow result merely because the two happened in the same season — loses because the meadow result is a premise, not a correlation the argument is trying to prove. The unsupported step is carrying that result over to the bog."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Ad hominem","d":1,"s":"Dr. Shah argues that the clinic should replace its entry rugs, and cites slip counts. A board member notes that Shah's sibling sells mats, and concludes from that note alone that the rugs need not be replaced.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["sets the slip counts aside because of a relative's business, rather than because of a problem in the counts","treats the sibling as a medical authority on entryway slips","infers that mats reduce slips merely because both were mentioned in the same month","generalizes from this clinic's entry to every clinic in the region","treats keeping the rugs and replacing them as if a third option had been ruled out by the sibling's job in this instance"],"a":0,"e":"The only reason given for dropping the slip counts is who would gain. That is a comment on Shah's household, not on the counts. The attractive choice — treats the sibling as a medical authority on entryway slips — loses because the sibling is not cited as an expert. The sibling's sales are cited as a reason to ignore Shah."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":1,"s":"Each instrument in the quintet is quiet enough for the chapel. Therefore, the quintet playing together is quiet enough for the chapel.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["treats being quiet enough as necessary for playing in the chapel and then as sufficient for being invited","rejects the choirmaster because the choirmaster rents instruments","treats the lack of a noise complaint as proof the quintet is quiet","gives the ensemble a feature established only for each instrument","projects last Sunday's volume onto next Sunday without a new fact"],"a":3,"e":"Five quiet instruments can be loud together. The chapel limit is applied to the ensemble just because it fits each player. The attractive choice — treats being quiet enough as necessary for playing in the chapel and then as sufficient for being invited — loses because the argument does not mention invitations or a rule for being asked to play. It moves from each instrument's volume to the group's volume."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":3,"s":"At the mill, oak was 40 percent of the boards shipped last year and 22 percent this year. The buyer concludes that fewer oak boards were shipped this year.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["uses \"oak\" for the tree in the premise and the color in the conclusion, as the argument states it","ignores the possibility that total boards shipped rose enough to offset oak's smaller share","treats last year's oak share as proof that the same share will return","gives the whole shipment a feature shown only for one board","dismisses the buyer because the buyer owns an oak lot"],"a":1,"e":"The argument reads a change in share as a change in number without settling what happened to the totals. The attractive choice — uses \"oak\" for the tree in the premise and the color in the conclusion, as the argument states it — loses because it names a mistake arguments often make, using this argument's subject, but it is not the step this argument takes."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":1,"s":"Every January rental on the lake is winterized. Cabin 9 is winterized. Therefore, cabin 9 is rented in January.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that winterizing caused the January rental market, because winterizing and January are both discussed","generalizes from cabin 9 to every cabin on the lake, though the conclusion names only cabin 9","rejects the rental office because the agent prefers summer leases","treats winterizing and leaving the cabin empty as the only two winter plans","treats a necessary condition for a January rental as if it were sufficient"],"a":4,"e":"January rental requires winterizing. The argument notices winterizing and concludes there is a January rental. The attractive choice — infers that winterizing caused the January rental market, because winterizing and January are both discussed — loses because the argument is about this cabin, not about what caused the rental market. The error is running \"January only if winterized\" backward."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Post hoc","d":1,"s":"The gallery hung a red curtain on Friday. On Saturday ticket sales fell. The manager concludes that the curtain caused the drop in sales.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["takes for granted that ticket sales cannot fall unless a curtain is changed","rejects the manager because the manager owns a fabric shop","treats the curtain as the cause of the sales drop solely because the curtain was hung first","generalizes from this gallery to every gallery in the city","reads a smaller share of Saturday sales as a smaller number without a total, though no share is given"],"a":2,"e":"The curtain went up, then sales fell. That order is the entire case for causation. The attractive choice — takes for granted that ticket sales cannot fall unless a curtain is changed — loses because the manager does not say a curtain change is required for any sales drop. The manager says this curtain caused this drop, and the only reason is the order of the two events."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":1,"s":"Courtyards that ran their fountains had more songbird visits the next spring than courtyards that left fountains off. The director concludes that the fountains caused the extra visits.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Nothing other than the fountains accounts for the difference in songbird visits.","Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the fountains caused the extra visits follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case.","Any courtyard that runs a fountain will gain songbirds, whatever else is true of the courtyard.","Most courtyards in the region have no fountain.","Most cases outside this one also fit the claim that the fountains caused the extra visits, whether or not they share its circumstances."],"a":0,"e":"The conclusion needs there to be no rival account of this gap. It does not need the much stronger claim that the factor works in every case no matter what else differs. The attractive choice — Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the fountains caused the extra visits follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":3,"s":"Seed packets fell from half the nursery's items sold to 30 percent. The buyer concludes that fewer seed packets were sold.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["The later total was smaller than the earlier total, not merely no larger, for the claim that fewer seed packets were sold.","Total items sold did not rise enough to offset seed packets' smaller share.","The nursery sold no items other than seed packets and flowering plants.","Most customers who buy seed packets buy nothing else.","Most cases outside this one also fit the claim that fewer seed packets were sold, whether or not they share its circumstances."],"a":1,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — The later total was smaller than the earlier total, not merely no larger, for the claim that fewer seed packets were sold. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":1,"s":"Dr. Feldman, author of the regional manual on harbor pilings, says the elm pilings should be replaced this year and offers no other reason. The board concludes that the elm pilings should be replaced this year.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the elm pilings should be replaced this year follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case.","Whatever Feldman says on any subject is a sufficient reason for the board to act.","Feldman's manual and training bear on whether these elm pilings should be replaced.","Several board members have not read the manual.","Most cases outside this one also fit the claim that the elm pilings should be replaced this year, whether or not they share its circumstances."],"a":2,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the elm pilings should be replaced this year follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":1,"s":"The orchard manager says: either the trees are netted, or the birds will ruin the crop. The trees will not be netted. Therefore, the birds will ruin the crop.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the birds will ruin the crop follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case.","Netting is the most effective bird control that exists for any crop.","Netting is the most expensive protection on the price list.","There is no way, other than netting, to keep the birds from ruining this crop.","Some fruit buyers prefer netted orchards."],"a":3,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the birds will ruin the crop follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Circular reasoning","d":3,"s":"The clerk says the clock-tower bell is on time because the bell is on time. From that remark alone, a listener concludes that the clerk's remark is adequate evidence for the claim.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the clerk's remark is adequate evidence for the claim follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case.","Most cases outside this one also fit the claim that the clerk's remark is adequate evidence for the claim, whether or not they share its circumstances.","The factor cited for the claim that the clerk's remark is adequate evidence for the claim is the factor the speaker hoped would matter.","The claim that the clerk's remark is adequate evidence for the claim was written down by someone who already accepted it.","A claim offered as its own only ground can be adequate evidence for that claim."],"a":4,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the clerk's remark is adequate evidence for the claim follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":1,"s":"On one midday crossing, most passengers who were asked said a dawn ferry should be added. The scheduler concludes that most of the ferry's passengers want a dawn trip.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["That midday crossing matches the ferry's passengers overall in the reasons they ride.","Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that most of the ferry's passengers want a dawn trip follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case.","Midday passengers are mostly tourists who would not board at dawn.","A few passengers on that crossing said no.","Most cases outside this one also fit the claim that most of the ferry's passengers want a dawn trip, whether or not they share its circumstances."],"a":0,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that most of the ferry's passengers want a dawn trip follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":1,"s":"No inspection log for the linen closet reports moths. The housekeeper concludes that the closet contains no moths.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the closet contains no moths follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case.","If the closet contained moths, an inspection would have reported them.","The housekeeper wrote some of the logs.","Most cases outside this one also fit the claim that the closet contains no moths, whether or not they share its circumstances.","The factor cited for the claim that the closet contains no moths is the factor the speaker hoped would matter."],"a":1,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the closet contains no moths follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":3,"s":"Every approved mural grant was reviewed by the arts board. This grant was reviewed by the arts board. Therefore, this grant was approved.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that this grant was approved describes, though not all of them do.","The result comes about only if the stated condition is met.","Every grant reviewed by the arts board was approved.","The premises were recorded accurately, which is all the argument has so far offered for the claim that this grant was approved.","Some further condition, never stated, would be enough for a result other than the claim that this grant was approved."],"a":2,"e":"The credited assumption is a conditional that, with the premises, forces the conclusion. A \"most\" claim does not force it. The attractive choice — Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that this grant was approved describes, though not all of them do. — loses because \"most,\" or a condition running in the other direction, does not force this conclusion from these premises."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":3,"s":"A phone ban reduced noise in the library reading room. Therefore, a phone ban will reduce noise in the hospital wards.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that a phone ban will reduce noise in the hospital wards describes, though not all of them do.","Some wards already ask visitors to speak softly, which says nothing about whether a reading-room ban will transfer.","The reading room is quieter than a hospital café.","Any phone ban that reduces noise in a reading room will reduce noise in these hospital wards.","Some wards already ask visitors to whisper."],"a":3,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that a phone ban will reduce noise in the hospital wards describes, though not all of them do. — loses because \"most,\" or a condition running in the other direction, does not force this conclusion from these premises."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":3,"s":"Each pump is under the quiet limit. Therefore, the fountain as a whole is under the quiet limit.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that the fountain as a whole is under the quiet limit describes, though not all of them do.","Residents near the plaza say they enjoy the sound of moving water.","The result comes about only if the stated condition is met.","The premises were recorded accurately, which is all the argument has so far offered for the claim that the fountain as a whole is under the quiet limit.","The fountain's noise equals the noise of its loudest pump."],"a":4,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that the fountain as a whole is under the quiet limit describes, though not all of them do. — loses because \"most,\" or a condition running in the other direction, does not force this conclusion from these premises."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Ignoring alternatives","d":3,"s":"The path was underwater this morning. Therefore, the irrigation line was left open overnight.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["The path is underwater in the morning only if the irrigation line was left open overnight.","Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that the irrigation line was left open overnight describes, though not all of them do.","An open irrigation line can leave a path underwater.","The irrigation line is painted green.","The result comes about only if the stated condition is met."],"a":0,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that the irrigation line was left open overnight describes, though not all of them do. — loses because \"most,\" or a condition running in the other direction, does not force this conclusion from these premises."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":3,"s":"Most maintainers of the east path want more gravel. Therefore, most users of the east path want more gravel.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that most users of the east path want more gravel describes, though not all of them do.","If most maintainers of the east path want more gravel, then most users of that path want more gravel.","Maintainers own the shovels used on the path.","Some users were not asked about gravel.","The path runs along the east creek."],"a":1,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that most users of the east path want more gravel describes, though not all of them do. — loses because \"most,\" or a condition running in the other direction, does not force this conclusion from these premises."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":1,"s":"Most passengers on one midday ferry crossing said they wanted a dawn trip. The scheduler concludes that most of the ferry's passengers want a dawn trip.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The people who were asked differ from the wider group on the very point at issue in the claim that most of the ferry's passengers want a dawn trip.","Only passengers who already commute at dawn were handed the question card.","Passengers on that crossing match the ferry's passengers overall in why they ride and how often they ride.","A few people on that crossing said the schedule should stay as it is.","What the argument already reports about the claim that most of the ferry's passengers want a dawn trip was recorded by one person, and no rival factor was measured."],"a":2,"e":"The conclusion steps from the people asked to a wider group. A match on the point at issue is what makes that step reasonable. The attractive choice — The people who were asked differ from the wider group on the very point at issue in the claim that most of the ferry's passengers want a dawn trip. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":1,"s":"No shift log mentions a jammed dome motor. The director concludes that the motor has not jammed.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The search behind the claim that the motor has not jammed looked where such an event is rarely written down, so a real instance could sit in a record that was not read.","The dome motor is louder than the projector fan.","The director wrote some of the shift logs.","Every jam of that motor would have been entered in the shift log, and the logs for the period are complete.","A jammed motor is expensive to replace."],"a":3,"e":"Silence supports absence only when an instance would have been recorded and the record was read. The attractive choice — The search behind the claim that the motor has not jammed looked where such an event is rarely written down, so a real instance could sit in a record that was not read. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":1,"s":"Each pump is under the quiet limit. The engineer concludes that the fountain as a whole is under that limit.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["Taken together, the parts go past the limit each part meets on its own, in the situation described by the claim that the fountain as a whole is under that limit.","The fountain is louder than its loudest pump because the basin adds its own noise.","The quiet limit was set by the town council rather than by the engineer.","What the argument already reports about the claim that the fountain as a whole is under that limit was recorded by one person, and no rival factor was measured.","The fountain's noise equals the noise of its loudest pump, and no other part of the fountain makes sound."],"a":4,"e":"If the whole does not exceed its most extreme part, that part meeting the limit settles the whole. The attractive choice — Taken together, the parts go past the limit each part meets on its own, in the situation described by the claim that the fountain as a whole is under that limit. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Ignoring alternatives","d":1,"s":"The cellar floor was wet this morning. The keeper concludes that the sump pump was left off overnight.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["No pipe burst, the floor dries within an hour of any spill, and a pump left off is the only other way this cellar floods.","The side that came out ahead was already ahead before any change, and that earlier gap is the one the records actually show for the claim that the sump pump was left off overnight.","The pump is painted the same green as the cellar door.","A pump left off can leave a cellar floor wet.","What the argument already reports about the claim that the sump pump was left off overnight was recorded by one person, and no rival factor was measured."],"a":0,"e":"A causal conclusion from a difference needs the sides to have been alike apart from that difference. The credited fact is the one that supplies that comparison. The attractive choice — The side that came out ahead was already ahead before any change, and that earlier gap is the one the records actually show for the claim that the sump pump was left off overnight. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Circular reasoning","d":3,"s":"The tide table is called reliable because it matches the harbormaster, who is called reliable because of the tide table. A clerk concludes that the table's reliability has been established.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["Checked against a standard that comes from neither source, the claim that the table's reliability has been established does not hold.","An independent gauge, calibrated last week and not set from either source, matches both the table and the harbormaster.","The table and the harbormaster agree with each other.","What the argument already reports about the claim that the table's reliability has been established was recorded by one person, and no rival factor was measured.","One side of the comparison in the claim that the table's reliability has been established paid more for equipment than the other side did."],"a":1,"e":"An independent check that agrees gives the conclusion a ground outside the circle. The attractive choice — Checked against a standard that comes from neither source, the claim that the table's reliability has been established does not hold. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":1,"s":"Shade cloth stopped leaf scorch on the herb beds. The gardener concludes that the same cloth will stop leaf scorch on the greenhouse benches.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The new setting does not work by the mechanism that produced the earlier result cited for the claim that the same cloth will stop leaf scorch on the greenhouse benches.","The greenhouse is larger than the herb beds.","The scorch in both places comes from the same midday sun on the same kind of leaves, and the cloth blocks that sun in both places.","Shade cloth is sold in several colors.","The herb beds were covered in June."],"a":2,"e":"The transfer is reasonable when the two settings work the same way. The credited fact says the mechanism matches. The attractive choice — The new setting does not work by the mechanism that produced the earlier result cited for the claim that the same cloth will stop leaf scorch on the greenhouse benches. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":1,"s":"The kiln reached the target temperature on every firing this month. The potter concludes that it will do so on the next firing.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The upcoming case differs, in the respect that mattered, from the past successes cited for the claim that it will do so on the next firing.","The potter bought a new apron before the next firing.","Some firings last year ran cool.","The next firing uses the same clay, the same load, and the same fuel setting as the firings that reached the target.","What the argument already reports about the claim that it will do so on the next firing was recorded by one person, and no rival factor was measured."],"a":3,"e":"A past run supports the next case only when that case is like the ones that worked. The credited fact says it is. The attractive choice — The upcoming case differs, in the respect that mattered, from the past successes cited for the claim that it will do so on the next firing. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":1,"s":"Anyone who finishes the dock course gets a green badge. Riley has a green badge. Therefore, Riley finished the course.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["That condition is the only way to reach the result, and it was met in the case the claim that Riley finished the course describes.","Riley was seen with dock-course materials the week the badge was issued.","The result in the claim that Riley finished the course was measured with one instrument on every part of the comparison.","The person drawing the claim that Riley finished the course expected that result before the comparison was made.","Green badges are also issued to inspectors who never take the course, and Riley is an inspector."],"a":4,"e":"There is another way to get the result, so the condition the argument cites is not required. The attractive choice — That condition is the only way to reach the result, and it was met in the case the claim that Riley finished the course describes. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Ignoring alternatives","d":1,"s":"The lane beside the greenhouse was underwater this morning. The gardener concludes that the drip line was left open overnight.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["A storm dropped three inches of rain overnight, and the drip valve stayed closed.","Before the change, the two sides already matched on the result and on the other factors relevant to the claim that the drip line was left open overnight, and the cited change was the only difference.","The gardener has worked in the greenhouse for many years.","The drip line is painted green to match the beds.","An open drip line can leave a lane underwater."],"a":0,"e":"The conclusion treats one difference as the reason the results diverged. The credited fact gives a rival reason, or shows the gap does not depend on that difference. The attractive choice — Before the change, the two sides already matched on the result and on the other factors relevant to the claim that the drip line was left open overnight, and the cited change was the only difference. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":1,"s":"A weekday matinee audience mostly called the play relaxing. The reviewer concludes that nearly all theatergoers find the play relaxing.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["On the question at issue, the people who were asked match the wider group named in the claim that nearly all theatergoers find the play relaxing.","Weekday matinees are mostly retirees, while the theater's other shows draw a younger crowd that has called the play tense.","A few matinee viewers did not answer the question.","The reviewer prefers plays described as relaxing.","The result in the claim that nearly all theatergoers find the play relaxing was measured with one instrument on every part of the comparison."],"a":1,"e":"If the people asked differ from the wider group on the point at issue, they do not stand for that group. The attractive choice — On the question at issue, the people who were asked match the wider group named in the claim that nearly all theatergoers find the play relaxing. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":1,"s":"A marathon champion says the city should fluoridate its drinking water. Therefore, the city should fluoridate its drinking water.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The person cited applied this field's own standard to this case before the claim that the city should fluoridate its drinking water was drawn.","Some residents dislike the taste of fluoridated water.","Regional water engineers studied this supply and recommended that it not be fluoridated.","The champion drinks this city's tap water every day.","A neighboring city fluoridates its drinking water."],"a":2,"e":"A source applying the proper standard points the other way, which undercuts a bare citation. The attractive choice — The person cited applied this field's own standard to this case before the claim that the city should fluoridate its drinking water was drawn. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Equivocation","d":3,"s":"A critic called the mural \"original,\" meaning a new design. The town protects an \"original,\" meaning the first physical panel. A councillor concludes the mural is protected because the critic called it original.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The source and the rule use the key word in one and the same sense in the argument for the claim that a critic called the mural \"original,\" meaning a new design.","The councillor personally admires the mural.","The result in the claim that a critic called the mural \"original,\" meaning a new design was measured with one instrument on every part of the comparison.","The critic was not talking about the first physical panel, and this mural is a later copy of an older design.","The person drawing the claim that a critic called the mural \"original,\" meaning a new design expected that result before the comparison was made."],"a":3,"e":"The two uses do not mean the same thing, so meeting one does not meet the other. The attractive choice — The source and the rule use the key word in one and the same sense in the argument for the claim that a critic called the mural \"original,\" meaning a new design. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":1,"s":"Towns that opened weekend markets saw honey sales rise in those same weeks. A clerk concludes that the markets caused the honey-sales rise.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Before the change, the two sides already matched on the result and on the other factors relevant to the claim that the markets caused the honey-sales rise, and the cited change was the only difference.","Some towns without markets sold a small amount of honey.","The clerk personally likes street markets.","The result in the claim that the markets caused the honey-sales rise was measured with one instrument on every part of the comparison.","Those same weeks were the only weeks the clover bloomed, and honey sales track the bloom even in towns with no market."],"a":4,"e":"The conclusion treats one difference as the reason the results diverged. The credited fact gives a rival reason, or shows the gap does not depend on that difference. The attractive choice — Before the change, the two sides already matched on the result and on the other factors relevant to the claim that the markets caused the honey-sales rise, and the cited change was the only difference. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":3,"s":"Most people who stayed on the museum's donation line supported a new wing. The fundraiser concludes that most visitors support a new wing.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Callers who stay on the donation line are already the museum's largest donors, and other visitors mostly oppose the wing.","On the question at issue, the people who were asked match the wider group named in the claim that most visitors support a new wing.","Some callers said they were in a hurry.","The result in the claim that most visitors support a new wing was measured with one instrument on every part of the comparison.","The person drawing the claim that most visitors support a new wing expected that result before the comparison was made."],"a":0,"e":"If the people asked differ from the wider group on the point at issue, they do not stand for that group. The attractive choice — On the question at issue, the people who were asked match the wider group named in the claim that most visitors support a new wing. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":3,"s":"A clerk may open the sealed folios only if that clerk wears linen gloves. Dr. Berg wears linen gloves today. The sealed folios include the 1912 mill map.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["Anyone wearing linen gloves may open the sealed folios, as the argument states it.","If Dr. Berg opens the 1912 mill map today, then Dr. Berg is wearing linen gloves.","The 1912 mill map may be opened without linen gloves.","Dr. Berg will open the 1912 mill map today.","Dr. Berg may open the sealed folios today."],"a":1,"e":"The credited statement is forced by the statements. The tempting one adds a cause, a reversed link, or a claim about other cases. The attractive choice — Anyone wearing linen gloves may open the sealed folios, as the argument states it. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":3,"s":"That a ferry ran without delays last summer does not, by itself, show that the same schedule will run without delays this summer. The memo's only reason for predicting no delays this summer is last summer's record.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["Last summer's weather will be repeated this summer.","The schedule will have delays this summer.","The memo's reason does not, by itself, show that the schedule will run without delays this summer.","The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that a ferry ran without delays last summer does not, by itself requires.","The same link will hold next year in every case like the claim that a ferry ran without delays last summer does not, by itself."],"a":2,"e":"The credited statement is forced by the statements. The tempting one adds a cause, a reversed link, or a claim about other cases. The attractive choice — Last summer's weather will be repeated this summer. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":3,"s":"A feature of each part need not be a feature of the whole. Each of the four cases is light enough for one person to carry. Nothing further is said about their combined weight.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The full set is too heavy for one person to carry.","One of the four cases is heavy.","The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that a feature of each part need not be a feature of the whole requires.","It does not follow that the full set is light enough for one person to carry.","The same link will hold next year in every case like the claim that a feature of each part need not be a feature of the whole."],"a":3,"e":"The credited statement is forced by the statements. The tempting one adds a cause, a reversed link, or a claim about other cases. The attractive choice — The full set is too heavy for one person to carry. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Conditional chain","d":3,"s":"If a grant is awarded, it is listed in the red ledger. If it is listed in the red ledger, the sponsor has filed a conflict form. Grant 12 has no conflict form on file. Grant 4 is listed in the red ledger.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["Grant 12 has a conflict form in a file other than the one described.","Grant 4's sponsor failed to file a conflict form.","No grant listed in the red ledger was awarded.","The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that if a grant is awarded, it is listed in the red ledger requires.","Grant 12 was not awarded."],"a":4,"e":"The credited statement is forced by the statements. The tempting one adds a cause, a reversed link, or a claim about other cases. The attractive choice — Grant 12 has a conflict form in a file other than the one described. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":3,"s":"The only people asked about a dawn ferry were passengers on one midday crossing, and most of them said yes. The scheduler has not compared that crossing with the rest of the ridership.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The midday answers do not yet settle whether most of the ferry's passengers want a dawn trip.","Most of the ferry's passengers want a dawn trip.","Most passengers oppose a dawn trip.","Midday passengers never commute.","The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that the only people asked about a dawn ferry were passengers on one midday crossing, requires."],"a":0,"e":"The credited statement stays inside what the information supports. The attractive choice — Most of the ferry's passengers want a dawn trip. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":3,"s":"The test used on the blue canoe would have revealed any hull crack, the whole hull was tested, and no crack was found.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that the test used on the blue canoe would have revealed any hull crack, the whole hull was tested, requires.","The test is positive evidence that the hull had no crack at the time of the test.","A crack was found and then left out of the report.","The test was not worth running.","The same link will hold next year in every case like the claim that the test used on the blue canoe would have revealed any hull crack, the whole hull was tested,."],"a":1,"e":"The credited statement stays inside what the information supports. The attractive choice — The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that the test used on the blue canoe would have revealed any hull crack, the whole hull was tested, requires. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":3,"s":"The fountain's noise equals the noise of its loudest pump, no other part makes sound, and every pump is under the town's quiet limit.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that the fountain's noise equals the noise of its loudest pump, no other part makes sound, requires.","The produce market beside the plaza is under the quiet limit.","The fountain as a whole is under the quiet limit.","Each pump is over the quiet limit.","The same link will hold next year in every case like the claim that the fountain's noise equals the noise of its loudest pump, no other part makes sound,."],"a":2,"e":"The credited statement stays inside what the information supports. The attractive choice — The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that the fountain's noise equals the noise of its loudest pump, no other part makes sound, requires. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":3,"s":"Green badges of this kind are issued only to people who finish the dock course. Riley holds one of those badges.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["Everyone who finished the dock course holds a green badge.","Riley is an inspector who skipped the course.","Riley will take the course next year.","Riley finished the dock course.","The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that green badges of this kind are issued only to people who finish the dock course requires."],"a":3,"e":"The credited statement stays inside what the information supports. The attractive choice — Everyone who finished the dock course holds a green badge. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Circular reasoning","d":3,"s":"An independent gauge, not set from the tide table or the harbormaster, matches both. The table and the harbormaster also match each other.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that an independent gauge, not set from the tide table or the harbormaster, matches both requires.","The table and the harbormaster prove each other without any outside check.","The gauge was set from the table.","The same link will hold next year in every case like the claim that an independent gauge, not set from the tide table or the harbormaster, matches both.","There is support for the table's reliability that does not rest only on the harbormaster."],"a":4,"e":"The credited statement stays inside what the information supports. The attractive choice — The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that an independent gauge, not set from the tide table or the harbormaster, matches both requires. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":1,"s":"Weekday matinees draw a narrower crowd than the theater's other shows. From a matinee poll alone, a reviewer concludes that nearly all theatergoers find the play relaxing. That poll is the reviewer's only evidence, and a narrower crowd does not establish the wider claim.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["The reviewer has not established that nearly all theatergoers find the play relaxing.","Weekday matinees draw a narrower crowd than the theater's other shows.","The play is relaxing for nearly all theatergoers.","Theatergoers never enjoy matinees.","The right response is to act at once on the claim that nearly all theatergoers find the play relaxing and to treat every similar case the same way."],"a":0,"e":"The main conclusion is the claim the other statements are offered to support. The attractive choice — Weekday matinees draw a narrower crowd than the theater's other shows. — loses because it is a premise, the denial of the conclusion, or a recommendation the argument does not draw."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":1,"s":"Last summer's delay-free season is the memo's only reason to predict a delay-free season this summer. A past season, by itself, does not establish that prediction.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["Last summer was delay-free.","The memo does not establish that this summer will be delay-free.","This summer will have delays.","Ferries are a kind of boat.","The memo was written in the spring, as the argument states it."],"a":1,"e":"The argument says the only reason on offer does not establish the prediction. It does not predict delays. The attractive choice — Last summer was delay-free. — loses because last summer is the premise. The conclusion is that the memo's prediction is not established. Turning the premise into the main point, or turning the gap into \"there will be delays,\" both miss the sentence the argument is driving at."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":1,"s":"Phone noise was the reading room's problem, and a ban reduced it. Ward noise is mostly monitors, and phones there are already silent. So the reading-room result is a poor reason to expect quieter wards.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["Ward noise is mostly monitors and carts.","A phone ban reduced reading-room noise.","The reading-room result is a poor reason to expect quieter wards.","Reading rooms and wards are identical.","The right response is to act at once on the claim that the reading-room result is a poor reason to expect quieter wards and to treat every similar case the same way."],"a":2,"e":"The main conclusion is the claim the other statements are offered to support. The attractive choice — Ward noise is mostly monitors and carts. — loses because it is a premise, the denial of the conclusion, or a recommendation the argument does not draw."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":1,"s":"Maintainers match users on the gravel question, and most maintainers want more gravel. So there is reason to think most users want more gravel.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["Maintainers match users on the gravel question.","Most maintainers want more gravel.","The right response is to act at once on the claim that there is reason to think most users want more gravel and to treat every similar case the same way.","There is reason to think most users want more gravel.","The evidence offered for the claim that there is reason to think most users want more gravel was gathered by the person who states it."],"a":3,"e":"The main conclusion is the claim the other statements are offered to support. The attractive choice — Maintainers match users on the gravel question. — loses because it is a premise, the denial of the conclusion, or a recommendation the argument does not draw."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"The reviewer polled only a weekday matinee and concludes that nearly all theatergoers find the play relaxing.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["treating a condition necessary for enjoying the play as if it were sufficient, as the argument states it","projecting a decade of full houses one year forward","showing that two reviews cite only each other","comparing a reading room with a hospital ward","drawing a conclusion about a large group from a narrower group that has not been shown to match it"],"a":4,"e":"The credited description is the move the argument makes. The attractive choice — treating a condition necessary for enjoying the play as if it were sufficient, as the argument states it — loses because it names a real technique, but not the one this argument uses."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"Either the pond is aerated or the fish will die. The pond will not be aerated. Therefore, the fish will die.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["treating two options as exhaustive and inferring the second from the rejection of the first","showing that each fish has a feature the whole pond may lack, as the argument states it","citing silence in a log as proof that the fish are healthy","matching a sample of ponds to every pond in the county","applying a specialist's manual to a measurement"],"a":0,"e":"The credited description is the move the argument makes. The attractive choice — showing that each fish has a feature the whole pond may lack, as the argument states it — loses because it names a real technique, but not the one this argument uses."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"A pastry chef says the cables should be replaced. Therefore, they should be replaced.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["fixing the number of cables so that a share drop becomes a count drop in this instance","treating an endorsement from outside the relevant field as sufficient for the decision","showing that a test would have found any crack in the cables","matching a sample of bridges to a national stock of bridges","offering a third way to hold the bridge together"],"a":1,"e":"The credited description is the move the argument makes. The attractive choice — fixing the number of cables so that a share drop becomes a count drop in this instance — loses because it names a real technique, but not the one this argument uses."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"No patrol note mentions a crack, so the seawall has no crack. Whether a crack would have been noted is not said.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["equating a midday sample of sailors with every user of the harbor","hiring a novelist to judge the wall","adding streetlights and counting collisions","carrying one case and then concluding about four","treating the absence of reports as proof of absence"],"a":4,"e":"Missing notes are the entire ground for \"no crack.\" Silence is being treated as a finding. The attractive choice — equating a midday sample of sailors with every user of the harbor — loses because nobody was sampled. The attractive choice is a sampling label on a silence argument. The ground is the missing notes."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":1,"s":"Weekday matinees draw a narrow crowd. **Nearly all theatergoers find the play relaxing.** That is what the reviewer concludes from one matinee.","q":"The statement in bold plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is evidence that the matinee was empty.","It is an objection to the reviewer.","It is the conclusion the reviewer draws.","It is a definition of \"matinee.\"","It is a premise offered in support of the argument's final conclusion, rather than that conclusion itself."],"a":2,"e":"The bold statement does the job the credited choice names. The attractive choice — It is evidence that the matinee was empty. — loses because it gives the bold statement a different job from the one it has. The usual trap is swapping premise and conclusion."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":1,"s":"**The trees will not be netted.** The manager says that either they are netted or the crop will be ruined, and concludes that the crop will be ruined.","q":"The statement in bold plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is the premise that rejects one of the two options.","It is the conclusion that the crop will be ruined in this instance.","It is a third option the manager offers.","It is a definition of netting.","It is evidence that the crop is already saved."],"a":0,"e":"The manager rejects netting and infers ruin. The bold sentence is that rejection, not the inference. The attractive choice — It is the conclusion that the crop will be ruined in this instance. — loses because \"will not be netted\" is easy to hear as the grim ending. The grim ending is ruin. Not netting is the rejected option, used as a premise."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":1,"s":"A pastry chef says the cables should be replaced. **The cables should be replaced.** The board draws that conclusion from the chef's remark alone.","q":"The statement in bold plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is a premise reporting the chef's professional duties.","It is an objection by an engineer.","It is a definition of \"cable.\"","It is the conclusion.","It is evidence that the chef declined to comment."],"a":3,"e":"The board concludes the bold sentence. The chef's remark is the premise. The attractive choice — It is a premise reporting the chef's professional duties. — loses because the replacement sentence is what a reader has just seen the chef say, so it is easy to leave it in the chef's mouth as background. The board adopts it as the conclusion. The chef's job is not what the sentence states."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":1,"s":"Principle: if a park tree is diseased and it threatens visitors, the city must remove it. The oak by the gate is diseased and threatens visitors. The elm by the pond is diseased and threatens no one. The maple is healthy.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The city must remove the elm by the pond.","The city must remove the oak by the gate.","The city must remove the maple.","The city must remove every tree in the park.","The city must not remove the oak."],"a":1,"e":"The oak meets both conditions, so the principle requires its removal. The elm misses the threat, and the maple misses both. The attractive choice — The city must remove the elm by the pond. — loses because the elm is diseased, which is half the principle, and disease is the word a quick reading chases. The principle also requires a threat to visitors. The elm has none. The oak has both."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":1,"s":"Principle: a past season of on-time ferries does not, by itself, establish that the next season will be on time. The memo predicts an on-time season solely because last season was on time.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The next season will be late.","The memo establishes the prediction, as the argument states it.","Ferries should be retired.","Last season was late.","The memo does not establish that the next season will be on time."],"a":4,"e":"Last season is the memo's only reason, and the principle says that reason does not establish the prediction. The attractive choice — The next season will be late. — loses because the principle says this sole reason fails. It does not predict lateness. The attractive choice converts a gap in proof into a forecast of delay."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":1,"s":"Principle: if the number of tickets stays the same and complaints' share of those tickets falls, then the number of complaints falls. Complaints' share fell, and the number of tickets stayed the same.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The number of complaints rose.","The number of tickets fell.","The number of complaints fell.","The share of complaints rose.","The desk received no tickets."],"a":2,"e":"Both conditions of the principle are met, so the number of complaints fell. The share did not rise, and the ticket total did not fall. The attractive choice — The number of complaints rose. — loses because a smaller share sounds, to a hasty arithmetic, as if it might hide a larger count. The principle says that when the total is fixed, the smaller share is a smaller count. \"Rose\" fights the principle."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":1,"s":"Principle: if a critic's only criticism is the author's motive, that criticism does not show the plan is unwise. Maren's only criticism of the reservoir plan is that its author wants to be reelected.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["No plan proposed by someone with a motive can be wise.","Maren's criticism shows the plan is unwise.","The result in the claim that if a critic's only criticism is the author's motive, that criticism does not show the plan is unwise was already in place before the factor the argument relies on.","Maren's criticism does not show that the reservoir plan is unwise.","A further record of the claim that if a critic's only criticism is the author's motive, that criticism does not show the plan is unwise was kept by the same office across the period the argument discusses."],"a":3,"e":"Applied as written, the principle yields the credited judgment and not the tempting one. The attractive choice — No plan proposed by someone with a motive can be wise. — loses because it is a judgment in the same setting, and the principle as stated does not yield it."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":1,"s":"Principle: if a test would have found any crack and the whole hull was tested, a negative result establishes that there was no crack then. That is what happened with the blue canoe yesterday.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The test establishes that the blue canoe's hull had no crack yesterday.","Every canoe in the boathouse was sound yesterday.","Negative results never establish anything.","The canoe can never crack later.","The result in the claim that if a test would have found any crack and the whole hull was tested was already in place before the factor the argument relies on."],"a":0,"e":"Applied as written, the principle yields the credited judgment and not the tempting one. The attractive choice — Every canoe in the boathouse was sound yesterday. — loses because it is a judgment in the same setting, and the principle as stated does not yield it."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":1,"s":"The market and honey sales rose together, and nothing else is cited. So the clerk has not established that the market caused the rise.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["If two things rise together, one of them caused the other.","Joint occurrence, by itself, does not establish causation.","Markets cause every nearby sales rise.","Clerks are authorities on causation.","Honey sales never change without a market."],"a":1,"e":"The argument treats the overlap as inadequate for a cause. That is the principle it uses. The attractive choice — If two things rise together, one of them caused the other. — loses because that is the rule the clerk hoped for and the argument denies. Similar subject, opposite direction. The argument's principle is that overlap is not enough."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":1,"s":"Netting will not be used, but reflective tape, which will be used, has saved similar crops. So it does not follow that the crop will be ruined.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["Orchard managers often dislike the look of reflective tape.","Skipping the most expensive option always ruins a crop.","Rejecting one protective option does not establish failure when another protective option is in place.","There are only two possible futures for any crop.","If the facts cited for the claim that it does not follow that the crop will be ruined merely occur together, that claim is established."],"a":2,"e":"The credited principle is the rule the argument is using. The attractive choice — Orchard managers often dislike the look of reflective tape. — loses because it is a nearby rule, often a stronger or reversed cousin of the rule the argument actually uses."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":1,"s":"The only support for replacing the cables is a pastry chef's remark, and the decision is about cables. So the replacement has not been established.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["Any famous person's remark establishes an engineering decision, as the argument states it.","Cables should be replaced whenever anyone comments on them.","Fame is the same thing as a relevant engineering standard.","An endorsement from outside the field of the decision does not, by itself, establish the decision.","Pastry chefs are cable experts."],"a":3,"e":"The credited principle is the rule the argument is using. The attractive choice — Any famous person's remark establishes an engineering decision, as the argument states it. — loses because it is a nearby rule, often a stronger or reversed cousin of the rule the argument actually uses."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":1,"s":"The log would have recorded any jam, it is complete, and it records none. So there was no jam.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["If the facts cited for the claim that there was no jam merely occur together, that claim is established.","Silence always establishes absence.","The claim that there was no jam is settled by the standing of the person who states it.","Whatever holds of each part of a case like the claim that there was no jam holds of the whole.","Silence establishes absence when any instance would have been recorded and the record is complete."],"a":4,"e":"The credited principle is the rule the argument is using. The attractive choice — If the facts cited for the claim that there was no jam merely occur together, that claim is established. — loses because it is a nearby rule, often a stronger or reversed cousin of the rule the argument actually uses."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":3,"s":"The library's reading room got noisier in the month after phones were banned there.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["The ban coincided with a ventilation fault louder than the phone noise it replaced, and the fault started that month.","The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that the library's reading room got noisier in the month after phones were banned there.","The library added more chairs, which do not themselves produce the kind of noise that increased.","The ban was announced a week before it took effect, and compliance was high.","Phones were banned in the reading room that month."],"a":0,"e":"The credited fact is the missing piece that lets both halves of the story be true together. The attractive choice — The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that the library's reading room got noisier in the month after phones were banned there. — loses because it confirms that the surprising half really happened, or it describes a different setting. It does not let both halves of this story stand together."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The archive that started opening previously unchecked drawers reported more silverfish the next quarter.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that the archive that started opening previously unchecked drawers reported more silverfish the next.","The newly opened drawers held silverfish that the old inspections, which never opened them, could not have reported.","The new inspections used the same definition of a silverfish finding as the old ones.","The archive's temperature and humidity were unchanged from the previous quarter.","Staff were asked to report silverfish, and they say they complied."],"a":1,"e":"The credited fact is the missing piece that lets both halves of the story be true together. The attractive choice — The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that the archive that started opening previously unchecked drawers reported more silverfish the next. — loses because it confirms that the surprising half really happened, or it describes a different setting. It does not let both halves of this story stand together."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The kiln that was serviced to hold a steadier temperature varied more from firing to firing after the service.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that the kiln that was serviced to hold a steadier temperature varied more from firing to firing after.","The service included the steps the kiln manual lists for steadier firing.","The service replaced a worn sensor with a miscalibrated one, and the potter followed the new sensor.","The potter used the same clay before and after the service.","The kiln was serviced before the variation increased."],"a":2,"e":"The credited fact is the missing piece that lets both halves of the story be true together. The attractive choice — The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that the kiln that was serviced to hold a steadier temperature varied more from firing to firing after. — loses because it confirms that the surprising half really happened, or it describes a different setting. It does not let both halves of this story stand together."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The museum that dropped its entry fee had fewer visitors the next month.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["Free entry is usually attractive to visitors.","The museum advertised the free month in the city paper.","The fee drop happened during the month the only road to the museum was closed for bridge work.","A visitor survey that month found the exhibits as well liked as in the paid month in this instance.","The museum did not shorten its opening hours."],"a":2,"e":"If people cannot reach the door, a lower fee does not increase visits. The closure lets the fee drop and the visit drop stand together. The attractive choice — Free entry is usually attractive to visitors. — loses because the usual pull of a free door is why the drop is surprising. A closed road explains fewer arrivals despite a more attractive price. The usual pull does not."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The historical society that digitized its diaries lent fewer diaries to researchers that year.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["Researchers used the digital copies, and the lending desk counts only physical loans.","The diaries were digitized that year.","The digital copies were harder to search than the physical diaries.","The society's membership, which supplies most researchers, grew that year in this instance.","The lending rules for physical diaries became more generous, not stricter."],"a":0,"e":"If use moved to copies and the desk counts only physical loans, the loan tally falls even though researchers are reading the diaries. The attractive choice — The diaries were digitized that year. — loses because digitizing is the setup. A shift to copies the desk does not count explains fewer recorded loans while access may have risen. The digitizing itself is not the resolution."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Parallel reasoning","d":3,"s":"The test would have found any crack, the whole hull was tested, and no crack was found. Therefore, there was no crack then.","q":"Which one of the following is most similar in its reasoning to the argument above?","c":["No jam is logged, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, there was no jam.","Any jam would have been logged, the log is complete, and no jam is logged. Therefore, there was no jam.","There was no jam, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, the log is complete.","The log mentions a jam, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, the canoe test would have found a crack.","Motors are loud, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, a visitor would notice a jam."],"a":1,"e":"Both say a complete sensitive check found nothing, and both conclude the thing was absent. The attractive choice — No jam is logged, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, there was no jam. — loses because silence alone is the flawed shortcut. The stimulus adds a complete test that would have caught the problem. The log choice that includes those two conditions matches. Silence by itself does not."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Parallel reasoning","d":3,"s":"This float is no heavier than floats the bridge has carried, and the bridge carried those floats. Therefore, there is reason to expect the bridge to carry this float.","q":"Which one of the following is most similar in its reasoning to the argument above?","c":["The kiln reached the target all month, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, it must reach the target next time, whatever the load on those facts alone.","The next firing will fail, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, past firings failed on those facts alone.","Cedar carries floats, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, every bridge is cedar on those facts alone.","A heavier float was carried once, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, every heavier float will be carried on those facts alone.","The next firing uses the same load and fuel as firings that reached the target, and those firings reached it. Therefore, there is reason to expect the next firing to reach the target."],"a":4,"e":"Both rely on a relevant match to past successes and conclude only that there is reason to expect success, not that success is guaranteed. The attractive choice — The kiln reached the target all month, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, it must reach the target next time, whatever the load on those facts alone. — loses because the trap drops the matched conditions and upgrades \"reason to expect\" into \"must.\" The stimulus keeps the match and claims only a reason to expect. The firing choice that keeps both matches."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Parallel reasoning","d":3,"s":"Last summer is the memo's only reason, and a past summer does not by itself establish the next one. Therefore, the memo does not establish a delay-free summer.","q":"Which one of the following is most similar in its reasoning to the argument above?","c":["The bridge carried ten floats, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, it will carry a heavier one on those facts alone.","The memo is about ferries, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, the bridge will fail on those facts alone.","A decade of lighter floats is the marshal's only reason, and that record does not by itself establish success with a heavier float. Therefore, the marshal does not establish that the bridge will carry this heavier float.","Heavier floats are unsafe, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, last summer had delays on those facts alone.","Any past success establishes any future success. No other support is offered, so the result is taken to follow in every later case of that kind on those facts alone."],"a":2,"e":"Both take a past record as the only reason, note that the record does not by itself establish the future claim, and conclude that the future claim is not established. The attractive choice — The bridge carried ten floats, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, it will carry a heavier one on those facts alone. — loses because the trap draws the future success the stimulus refuses to draw. The marshal choice, like the memo, stops at \"does not establish.\""},
{"t":"Parallel flaw","pattern":"Parallel flaw","d":3,"s":"Honey sales and the weekend market rose in the same weeks. Therefore, the market caused the rise.","q":"Which one of the following exhibits flawed reasoning most similar to that in the argument above?","c":["Lantern sales rose after the harbor was closed, and the closure was the only change that week. Therefore, the closure is a candidate cause.","The concerts were canceled, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, lantern sales will fall.","Each lantern is light, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, a crate of lanterns is light.","Lantern sales and the evening concerts rose in the same weeks. Therefore, the concerts caused the lantern rise.","The result in the claim that the market caused the rise was already in place before the factor the argument relies on."],"a":3,"e":"The credited argument repeats the same kind of mistake. The attractive choice — Lantern sales rose after the harbor was closed, and the closure was the only change that week. Therefore, the closure is a candidate cause. — loses because it shares a topic or a few words, but not the structure, or it repairs the mistake."},
{"t":"Parallel flaw","pattern":"Parallel flaw","d":3,"s":"A famous chess champion says the town dam should be lowered. Therefore, the town dam should be lowered.","q":"Which one of the following exhibits flawed reasoning most similar to that in the argument above?","c":["The author of the dam manual applies the manual's rule to this dam's measurements. Therefore, there is relevant support for following that rule.","The dam is concrete, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, a chess player should not comment.","Either the dam is lowered or the town floods. It will not be lowered. Therefore, the town floods.","A marathon champion says the tap water should be fluoridated. Therefore, it should be fluoridated.","The champion's remark matches an engineer's measurements. Therefore, the remark is not the only support."],"a":3,"e":"Both treat a celebrated non-specialist's say-so as enough for a technical decision. The manual application does not. The attractive choice — The author of the dam manual applies the manual's rule to this dam's measurements. Therefore, there is relevant support for following that rule. — loses because the trap supplies a relevant standard. The stimulus and the fluoridation choice rely on an outsider alone. A manual applied to measurements is not that flaw."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":2,"s":"The bridge carried ten years of floats. The marshal concludes that it will carry this year's float.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether the person drawing the claim that it will carry this year's float would gain personally if that claim were accepted.","Whether a written notice of the claim that it will carry this year's float was posted where the audience could read it.","Whether acting on the claim that it will carry this year's float would be expensive if the claim turned out to be wrong.","Whether a similar claim was accepted last year in a setting this argument does not discuss.","Whether this year's float is as easy on the bridge as the floats already carried"],"a":4,"e":"The useful question is the one whose answers pull the conclusion in opposite directions. The credited question is aimed at the gap. The attractive choice — Whether the person drawing the claim that it will carry this year's float would gain personally if that claim were accepted. — loses because it can be answered either way without deciding whether the evidence reaches the conclusion."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":2,"s":"A clipboard cut lost badges at the craft museum. The director concludes that it will do so at the science hall.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether badges are lost for the same reason, at a similarly staffed desk, in both places","Whether the person drawing the claim that it will do so at the science hall would gain personally if that claim were accepted.","Whether the director's title includes the word \"chief\"","Whether a written notice of the claim that it will do so at the science hall was posted where the audience could read it.","Whether acting on the claim that it will do so at the science hall would be expensive if the claim turned out to be wrong."],"a":0,"e":"The useful question is the one whose answers pull the conclusion in opposite directions. The credited question is aimed at the gap. The attractive choice — Whether the person drawing the claim that it will do so at the science hall would gain personally if that claim were accepted. — loses because it can be answered either way without deciding whether the evidence reaches the conclusion."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":2,"s":"Either the gallery hires a night guard or the drawings will be stolen. It will not hire a night guard. Therefore, the drawings will be stolen.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether the person drawing the claim that the drawings will be stolen would gain personally if that claim were accepted.","Whether the drawings can be kept safe by some measure other than a night guard","Whether a written notice of the claim that the drawings will be stolen was posted where the audience could read it.","Whether acting on the claim that the drawings will be stolen would be expensive if the claim turned out to be wrong.","Whether a similar claim was accepted last year in a setting this argument does not discuss."],"a":1,"e":"The useful question is the one whose answers pull the conclusion in opposite directions. The credited question is aimed at the gap. The attractive choice — Whether the person drawing the claim that the drawings will be stolen would gain personally if that claim were accepted. — loses because it can be answered either way without deciding whether the evidence reaches the conclusion."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":2,"s":"A phone ban reduced reading-room noise. The director concludes that a phone ban will reduce ward noise.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether the person drawing the claim that a phone ban will reduce ward noise would gain personally if that claim were accepted.","Whether the hospital building is larger than the library","Whether the library bought new chairs that month","Whether ward noise has the same source the ban reduced in the reading room","Whether a written notice of the claim that a phone ban will reduce ward noise was posted where the audience could read it."],"a":3,"e":"The useful question is the one whose answers pull the conclusion in opposite directions. The credited question is aimed at the gap. The attractive choice — Whether the person drawing the claim that a phone ban will reduce ward noise would gain personally if that claim were accepted. — loses because it can be answered either way without deciding whether the evidence reaches the conclusion."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":3,"s":"Chen: The matinee poll shows that nearly all theatergoers find the play relaxing. Duran: The matinee poll shows what that matinee thought, not what nearly all theatergoers think.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["the matinee both speakers mention was actually under discussion","one of the two speakers has a personal stake in how the question about the matinee comes out","every dispute of this kind has to be resolved the way the speakers resolve the matinee","the matinee had never come up before these two speakers addressed it","the matinee poll shows that nearly all theatergoers find the play relaxing"],"a":4,"e":"The speakers are committed to opposite answers on the credited point, and not on the tempting one. The attractive choice — the matinee both speakers mention was actually under discussion — loses because both speakers can accept it, or neither has taken a stand on it. Disagreement needs opposite commitments."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":3,"s":"Ida: The clipboard that cut losses at the craft museum will cut losses at the science hall. Jules: The craft museum losses were from an unattended desk, and the science hall losses are from loading-dock theft, so the clipboard result does not carry over.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["the craft museum clipboard result shows that the science hall will lose fewer badges","the clipboard both speakers mention was actually under discussion","one of the two speakers has a personal stake in how the question about the clipboard comes out","every dispute of this kind has to be resolved the way the speakers resolve the clipboard","the clipboard had never come up before these two speakers addressed it"],"a":0,"e":"The speakers are committed to opposite answers on the credited point, and not on the tempting one. The attractive choice — the clipboard both speakers mention was actually under discussion — loses because both speakers can accept it, or neither has taken a stand on it. Disagreement needs opposite commitments."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":3,"s":"Ruth: There are no silverfish, because no inspection reported any. Sam: The inspections never opened the map drawers, so the lack of reports does not show that there are no silverfish.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["the silverfish, both speakers mention was actually under discussion","the lack of reports shows that there are no silverfish","one of the two speakers has a personal stake in how the question about the silverfish, comes out","every dispute of this kind has to be resolved the way the speakers resolve the silverfish,","the silverfish, had never come up before these two speakers addressed it"],"a":1,"e":"The speakers are committed to opposite answers on the credited point, and not on the tempting one. The attractive choice — the silverfish, both speakers mention was actually under discussion — loses because both speakers can accept it, or neither has taken a stand on it. Disagreement needs opposite commitments."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":3,"s":"Yara: The tent is a way to hold the festival, so it is false that the only options are canceling it or moving it into the hall. Zev: Canceling it and moving it into the hall are the only options, and the tent is not a way to hold the festival.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["the festival, both speakers mention was actually under discussion","canceling a festival is something a person might propose","the tent is a way to hold the festival","one of the two speakers has a personal stake in how the question about the festival, comes out","every dispute of this kind has to be resolved the way the speakers resolve the festival,"],"a":2,"e":"The speakers are committed to opposite answers on the credited point, and not on the tempting one. The attractive choice — the festival, both speakers mention was actually under discussion — loses because both speakers can accept it, or neither has taken a stand on it. Disagreement needs opposite commitments."}
]);
